    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d498">

                <pb facs="0579" n="467" sameAs="frus:frus1919Russia/pg467"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.00/3800: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Reinsch</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content" calendar="gregorian"
                            notAfter="1919-02-04T08:35:00-05:00"
                            notBefore="1919-02-03T14:00:00+04:53"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >undated</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received February 4, 1919, 8:35
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>Following from [Harris at] Omsk.</p>
                <p>“83, February 3, 2 p.m. Government has asked that an American officer be attached
                    to commission which is proceeding Chita to inquire into
                        Semenoff conduct. French and English representatives
                    here will comply providing other interested powers do so. I have referred
                    request to General Graves through
                    Captain Schuyler.<anchor n="21" xml:id="d498-f1"/> I understand this whole
                    commission is to sit [under?] Japanese protection. Harris.”</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Reinsch</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d498-f1" n="21" xml:id="d498-f1-content">
                <p>Capt. Montgomery Schuyler, U.S.A., on duty at Omsk.</p>
